---
import BlogLayout from '../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro';
import CopyButton from '../../components/CopyButton.astro';
import { authoredBlogMetadata, formatBlogDate } from '../../data/blogPresentation';
import { createBreadcrumbSchema, productFacts } from '../../data/productFacts';

const title = 'True TDD for Google Cloud With Watch Mode';
const description = 'Run pytest or jest --watch against real Google Cloud SDKs without slow network calls or cloud bills by resetting local project state between test saves.';
const pageUrl = new URL('blog/tdd-google-cloud-watch-mode/', productFacts.siteUrl).toString();
const metadata = authoredBlogMetadata['tdd-google-cloud-watch-mode'];
const contents = [
  { id: 'problem', label: 'Why cloud code skips test-driven development' },
  { id: 'solution', label: 'Fast red-green-refactor with local resets' },
  { id: 'fixture', label: 'Wire a pytest fixture to /reset' },
  { id: 'try-it', label: 'Run the watch-mode loop' },
  { id: 'scope', label: 'Keep tests deterministic and scoped' },
];
const pytestHook = `import os
import urllib.request
import pytest

@pytest.fixture(autouse=True)
def reset_localcloud_state():
    # Uses the gateway URL or default port 5380 to clear project state (~380 ms)
    gateway = os.environ.get("LOCALCLOUD_GATEWAY_URL", "http://localhost:5380")
    req = urllib.request.Request(
        f"{gateway}/reset?project=local-gcp-project",
        data=b'{"restore_seed":true}',
        headers={"Content-Type": "application/json"},
        method="POST",
    )
    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=5) as resp:
        assert resp.status == 200`;
const watchCmd = `localcloud start --local-only
eval "$(localcloud env)"
# Re-run integration tests automatically on every file save
pytest-watch tests/integration/`;
const structuredData = [
  createBreadcrumbSchema([
    { name: 'Home', url: productFacts.siteUrl },
    { name: 'Blog', url: new URL('blog/', productFacts.siteUrl).toString() },
    { name: title, url: pageUrl },
  ]),
];
---

<BlogLayout {title} {description} {structuredData} {contents} article={{ publishedAt: metadata.publishedAt, headline: title }}>
  <Fragment slot="hero">
    <article class="site-section site-section--hero">
      <div class="container container--narrow">
        <nav class="mb-5 text-sm text-[var(--text-2)]" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/blog/">Blog</a></nav>
        <span class="site-eyebrow">Developer Productivity</span>
        <h1 class="mt-5">{title}</h1>
        <p class="blog-meta"><span>Published <time datetime={metadata.publishedAt}>{formatBlogDate(metadata.publishedAt)}</time></span><span>{metadata.minutes} min read</span><span>{metadata.topic}</span></p>
        <p class="blog-deck">File-watching test runners fail against live cloud projects due to API latency, rate limits, and leftover state. LocalCloud makes watch-mode TDD practical against real Google Cloud client libraries.</p>
        <div class="blog-actions">
          <a class="site-button site-button--primary" href="/docs/sdk-examples/">SDK examples</a>
          <a href="/docs/">Quick start</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>
  </Fragment>

  <section class="site-section">
    <div class="container container--narrow space-y-10">
      <section>
        <h2 id="problem">Why cloud code skips test-driven development</h2>
        <p class="mt-4">Developers rely on <code>jest --watch</code> or <code>pytest-watch</code> for pure unit logic because feedback takes milliseconds. The moment code touches Cloud Storage, Pub/Sub, or Spanner, watch mode breaks down.</p>
        <p class="mt-4">Hitting live GCP on every file save triggers rate limits, accumulates orphan buckets and topics, and costs money per request. Teams either mock away the SDK—hiding real wire-format bugs—or push cloud integration tests to slow CI pipelines.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 id="solution">Fast red-green-refactor with local resets</h2>
        <p class="mt-4">With LocalCloud running on localhost, each API call completes in 2–7 ms and costs $0. Crucially, LocalCloud exposes a project-scoped reset endpoint (<code>POST /reset?project=local-gcp-project</code>) that clears project data in roughly 380 ms while keeping the container warm.</p>
        <p class="mt-4">Put that call in a test setup hook and your watch runner gets a fresh, empty—or freshly seeded—Google Cloud environment on every save.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 id="fixture">Wire a pytest fixture to /reset</h2>
        <p class="mt-4">Add an <code>autouse</code> fixture in <code>conftest.py</code> (or a <code>beforeEach</code> hook in Jest/Vitest) so tests never inherit stale buckets or unread messages from the previous save:</p>
        <div class="site-panel mt-5">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-4">
            <span class="site-kicker">conftest.py</span>
            <CopyButton text={pytestHook} analyticsLabel="copy-blog-tdd-fixture" />
          </div>
          <pre><code>{pytestHook}</code></pre>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 id="try-it">Run the watch-mode loop</h2>
        <p class="mt-4">Start LocalCloud, export your local environment, and launch your watcher. Check <a href="/docs/">Getting Started</a> for CLI installation and <a href="/docs/seed-data/">Seed Data</a> to understand <code>restore_seed</code> options.</p>
        <div class="site-panel mt-5">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-4">
            <span class="site-kicker">Watch-mode commands</span>
            <CopyButton text={watchCmd} analyticsLabel="copy-blog-tdd-watch" />
          </div>
          <pre><code>{watchCmd}</code></pre>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 id="scope">Keep tests deterministic and scoped</h2>
        <p class="mt-4">Use local watch mode to drive out application logic, schema queries, and error paths quickly. Pair it with a <a href="/docs/github-actions/">GitHub Actions CI check</a> and final staging validation on Google Cloud before release.</p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </section>
</BlogLayout>
